ABL="Ableton/MIDI-friendly: device names map to stock Live devices or generic modules; patterns are plain MIDI clips."
EARLY="If the track has drums, they enter early (~8 counts / ~12 s); only pad/texture before that (Matt's preference)."

# 1 ---------------------------------------------------------------- stereo width
S("LcAcmBKryxE","Stereo Width Part 2 - Widening",
 "correlation-meter-widening-haas-comb-fail-stereo-phaser-ms-side-gain-linear-vs-detune-chorus-multiband-width-map",
 "Correlation-meter-guided widening: Haas fails mono (comb filter), stereo phaser + M/S side gain, linear vs detuning chorus, and a per-band width map (mono lows, moderate low-mids, widest upper mids, restrained highs)",
 "Widen a mono or narrow synth/pad while staying mono-safe, judging every move on a correlation meter/vectorscope (+1 = mono, 0 = fully independent L/R = widest safe, below 0 = cancels in mono). Reject pure Haas widening because a fixed one-channel delay is a comb filter when summed to mono. Create stereo with a stereo phaser (different delay/filtering per channel) and then scale width with mid/side side-gain. Choose linear (non-detuning) chorus for natural instruments/classic synths and detuning (supersaw-style) chorus to thicken thin digital synths. Finish with a multiband width map: lows (below ~100-300 Hz) mono, low-mids up to ~1.6 kHz slightly narrower, upper mids widest (correlation touching but not crossing 0), highs/transients not too wide. Delta vs the stereo-width cluster: the correlation-meter target per band and the phaser-then-M/S widening path.",
 ["A DAW with a correlation meter / vectorscope on the bus or track","A stereo phaser, a chorus (linear) and a detune/unison-style chorus, a mid/side utility, a multiband (3-4 band) stereo width tool","A mono-sum utility for checks"],
 [("source","audio","Mono or narrow synth/pad/lead to widen",True),("mix_context","audio","Rest of the mix with bass and drums, for the final check",False)],
 [("widened_part","audio","Wider part whose correlation stays >= 0 and that holds its tone in mono"),("width_map","notes","Band split points and width per band used")],
 ["Correlation is the measure: never let the bus or the widened part sit below 0 for long; below 0 means the channels cancel when summed to mono.",
  "Pure Haas (fixed delay on one channel) is rejected here: mono-summed it is a comb filter with a fixed notch pattern the ear spots as unnatural.",
  "Chorus modulates delay time, so keep depth subtle on sustained pads so pitch stays steady (Matt dislikes wobbly pitch); prefer the phaser + side-gain route on held chords.",
  "Band split values (~100-300 Hz, ~1.6 kHz) are the presenter's personal starting points, not universal; tune by ear and meter.",
  "Best width comes from wide source material (e.g. real double-takes); these tools only create or rebalance width from a mono/narrow source.",ABL],
 [("Put a correlation meter / vectorscope on the part. Read it: +1 = identical channels (mono), 0 = fully independent (maximum safe width), negative = opposite polarity that will cancel in mono. Mono-sum a very wide source to hear the change (a negative-correlation choir changed tone audibly; a moderately wide supersaw barely changed).","Correlation meter + mono utility","You can predict mono loss from the meter"),
  ("Test pure Haas: delay one channel by a fixed few ms, then mono-sum. Hear the comb-filter colour (it behaves like a comb-filter device). Do not use this as the widening method.","Delay on one channel","Haas recognised as not mono-compatible"),
  ("Create real stereo from mono with a stereo phaser (default settings are a fine start): each channel gets different delay/filtering, so the meter leaves +1 while keeping the phaser character modest.","Stereo phaser","Correlation drops below +1; part is now stereo"),
  ("Scale the width with mid/side: split into mid (common to L and R) and side (difference), and raise or lower only the side gain. Compare wet/dry: the core tone should stay the same because only existing content is boosted or cut.","Mid/side utility (side gain)","Width adjustable without changing the sound's heart"),
  ("Pick the chorus type: a linear (non-detuning) chorus for natural instruments or classic synth leads, which only gets a bit quieter in mono; a detuning/unison-style spread chorus for thin, harsh digital synths that need thickening (it changes a little in mono but does not collapse).","Chorus (linear vs detune)","Width + thickness matched to the source type"),
  ("Apply a multiband width map: split at ~100 Hz (try 100-300 Hz) and keep that band mono; low-mids up to ~1.6 kHz a little narrower but still solid; upper mids the widest, pushed until correlation touches 0 but does not cross it; highs (mostly transients) not too wide so they do not vanish in mono.","Multiband stereo width tool","Part reads wide in a full mix while the meter stays >= 0"),
  ("Check in the full mix with bass and drums and in mono, then trim band widths.","Mix + mono check","Wide but mono-safe part")],
 ["Correlation meter on the processed part stays >= 0 throughout playback.","Band below the low split reads ~+1 (mono).","Mono sum shows no comb-filter notch pattern and level drop of the part is small (about 3 dB or less)."],
 ["stereo-width","correlation","mid-side","phaser","chorus","multiband","mono-compatibility","haas"])

# 2 ---------------------------------------------------------------- delays / loops
S("VSGYqvV4bTg","Get Creative with 6 Unexpected Uses for Ableton's Delays",
 "delay-as-instrument-mono-checked-haas-sweet-spot-delay-built-chorus-flanger-100-feedback-repitch-octave-looper-grain-delay-octave-shift",
 "Delay as instrument: mono-checked Haas sweet spot, chorus/flanger built from a modulated delay, a 100%-feedback analog-style looper whose delay time is halved/doubled for octave steps, and a 1 ms grain delay as an octave shifter",
 "Use ordinary delay devices for loops and colour rather than echo. (1) Haas width on a clap/short part, with the delay time tuned while mono-summed to find the sweet spot. (2) Chorus (~20 ms, moderate modulation, LFOs out of phase, feedback <= ~60%) and flanger (shorter time, higher feedback, much deeper modulation, stereo) built from a delay. (3) Analog-style looper: delay at its longest synced time, feedback exactly 100%, overdub new notes each cycle, use repitch mode to halve/double the time for clean octave steps (stepping up in the second half of the loop to build energy), kill with feedback 0, then resample to audio. (4) Grain delay at 1 ms / feedback 0 / +12 st as an octave shifter. Delta vs reverb-delay cluster: the overdub looper with octave-step repitch and the resample-to-layer workflow (loops gap).",
 ["A DAW with a stereo delay that has unlinked L/R time, feedback, a repitch/tape mode and sync; a modulated echo/delay; a grain delay; a mono utility","An audio track to resample onto"],
 [("source_part","midi|audio","Synth notes or chords to feed the delay/looper",True),("tempo","number","Project tempo (the loop length follows the synced delay time)",True)],
 [("loop_layer","audio","Resampled looper texture placed under the main pattern"),("fx_racks","preset","Haas, chorus, flanger, looper and octave-shift delay setups")],
 ["Looper octave changes must be clean doubling/halving of the delay time (octave steps), not continuous random delay-time modulation: the transcript's random-LFO tape repitch and the echo 'wobble' control are dropped for Matt (no wobbly random pitch).",
  "Grain-delay octave shift: keep spray moderate; if grains start ticking (a rapid clicking texture Matt dislikes), lower spray/frequency or the wet amount.",
  "Haas on short parts only and always mono-checked; too short a delay = phasey ringing and lost attack, too long = audible echo.",
  "Feedback at exactly 100% builds level over overdubs; watch the meter and add a limiter or trim after the looper.",ABL,EARLY],
 [("Haas: on a clap (or other short part) unlink the delay channels, time mode, left as low as possible, right ~15 ms, feedback 0, filter off; raise dry/wet. Add a utility after it set to mono and adjust the right delay time while mono until the part keeps its attack (no ringing, no audible echo). Then disable mono.","Delay + utility (mono)","Wider clap that still sounds like a clap in mono"),
  ("Chorus from a delay: turn off filter, modulation and character, feedback 0, wet max, channels linked in time mode at ~20 ms. Raise delay modulation to moderate and set the LFO phase offset to anything but 0. Feedback up to ~60% exaggerates it. Optional colour: reverb in the feedback path, ping-pong, input drive, the delay's filter.","Echo-type delay","Lush, wide chords"),
  ("Flanger from the same delay: a much shorter time, higher feedback, much higher modulation depth (a x4 depth switch helps), stereo mode. Use subtly on drums for liveliness.","Echo-type delay","Analog-style flanging"),
  ("Looper: put a delay on the synth track, filter/modulation/wobble off, both channels linked in sync mode, delay time at the maximum, feedback exactly 100%. Play notes: they repeat forever; play new notes on each cycle to overdub.","Delay as looper","A self-sustaining loop built by overdubs"),
  ("With the delay in repitch mode, halve or double the delay time to move the whole loop up or down an octave (each change also shifts the tone slightly). Build energy by stepping it up a few octaves in the second half of the loop. Stop the loop by setting feedback to 0; it dies out after the next cycle.","Repitch mode + delay time","Octave-stepped loop with an energy lift"),
  ("Resample the looper output to a new audio track and place it under the main pattern as a texture layer.","Resample to audio","Committed loop layer in the arrangement"),
  ("Octave shifter: grain delay in time mode at 1 ms, feedback 0, pitch +12 st, spray and frequency at moderate values; raise dry/wet, then tune spray/frequency for tone. Automate pitch amount and dry/wet in a clip to add life to a repetitive short loop.","Grain delay","Octave-up layer that refreshes a repeating loop")],
 ["Haas part: mono sum keeps the transient (peak level within a few dB of the stereo version).","Looper layer: octave changes land on exact 2:1 time ratios (pitch moves in clean 12-semitone steps).","Resampled loop layer has no level runaway (peak below 0 dBFS with headroom)."],
 ["delay","looper","repitch","chorus","flanger","haas","grain-delay","octave","resample","loops"])

# 3 ---------------------------------------------------------------- ambient arrangement
S("CW3jk3BgE1E","Learn to make ambient music in 4 simple steps",
 "ambient-variation-sets-per-voice-presence-focus-map-frequency-slot-plan-texture-pad-melody",
 "Ambient track from variation sets: give each voice (texture, pad, melody) lettered variations (filter low/mid/high, modulation depth), chain them along a presence/focus map, and plan frequency slots so layers never fight",
 "Build an evolving ambient track from three voices (a looping sample texture, a slow paraphonic pad, a floating repetitive melody) without a muddy, aimless wall of reverb. (1) Start with one sound and one reverb and develop a set of variations (A, B, C...) using filters (driven low, wide mid, thin high) and attenuated slow modulation. (2) Chain variations within each voice to tell a story, with slow automatic modulation adding depth but not changing the voice's role. (3) Draw a presence/focus map (focus, not volume) for the three voices across the track and assign a variation per section. (4) Match it to a low/mid/high frequency-slot map and resolve clashes by switching variations or EQ. Delta vs arrangement-ambient cluster: the per-voice variation set and focus-not-volume map tied to a frequency-slot plan.",
 ["A synth or modular with 2 oscillators, mixer, high-pass + low-pass filters, a decay envelope and a step sequencer (two sequences of different lengths)","A sampler/looper with a band-pass filter","LFOs / smooth random sources with attenuators (or mod-matrix depth controls)","One reverb; a mixer with 3-band EQ per channel"],
 [("pad_notes","midi","Two step sequences, 3 and 4 steps long, for the pad's two oscillators",True),("melody","midi","Short repetitive melody sequence",True),("texture_loop","audio","Short looping sample/field texture",True)],
 [("variation_sheet","notes","Per voice: lettered variations with filter/mod settings"),("focus_map","notes","Section-by-section focus and assigned variation per voice"),("track","audio","Rendered ambient track")],
 ["Use a single reverb while exploring; build depth from the core sounds rather than effects.",
  "Every modulation goes through an attenuator so variations blend smoothly.",
  "Dropped for Matt: the transcript's smooth-random modulation of the sample playback speed (pitch wobble). Move the texture with filter cutoff/band-pass only; keep pitch steady.",
  "Slow modulation is timbre-only (wave shape, filter, FM depth via VCA) and subtle; it adds depth but must not move the voice to a different role or register.",EARLY,ABL],
 [("Pick one inspiring sound and stay with it; use only one reverb. Aim for a sound with depth and a set of lettered variations (A, B, C...).","Exploration","Variation list started for voice 1"),
  ("Pad: two oscillators into a mixer, then high-pass and low-pass filters. Feed the oscillators two different step sequences (3 steps and 4 steps) for a paraphonic polymetric pad; a gate into a decay envelope opens the low-pass. Make filter variations: driven low, wide mid-range, thin high.","Pad patch","Three pad variations from filters alone"),
  ("Add modulation variations through attenuators: slow smooth random on the wavetable shape of one oscillator (works best on the low variation); an LFO through a VCA into the FM input of the other (works best on mid and high variations).","Mod sources + attenuators","Pad can morph smoothly between variations"),
  ("Melody voice: single oscillator, high-pass + low-pass, sequencer and decay envelope, with attenuated LFOs on filter spread and wave shape. Texture voice: looping sample through a band-pass filter. Make front/back variations of each (push back with the filter, bring forward with modulation; texture from deep modulated rumble to bright crispy top).","Melody + texture patches","Each voice can move between foreground and background"),
  ("Draw a presence/focus map (focus, not volume) for the three voices. Example from the transcript: open on a present sample texture (solo intro carrying the track), pad enters and pushes the texture back, both step back when the melody enters, melody in focus for a while, pad and noise come back up, then all calm to a slow outro.","Sketch / notebook","Section plan showing which voice is in focus when"),
  ("Assign a variation to each section: a deep, heavily modulated texture variation to carry the intro, a background variation while the pad is central, a bright low-cut noise variation when the melody is focal. A focal sound needs depth and weight; a background sound should be pleasant without effort.","Variation per section","Every section has a defined variation per voice"),
  ("Make a frequency-slot map (low / mid / high) beside the focus map. When two voices want the same slot (dark rumbling texture + dark pad), either use a brighter pad variation with little low end, make the texture thin and high from the start, or cut the texture's lows before/while the low pad enters. Use a mixer's 3-band EQ as a playable variation control, not set-and-forget.","Frequency map + 3-band EQ","Layers sit in separate slots; no mud when voices overlap"),
  ("Perform/automate the main evolution by hand along the focus map, letting the slow automatic modulation keep background voices alive; render.","Arrangement / performance","Evolving track that tells a story")],
 ["Each voice has >= 3 named variations recorded on the variation sheet.","Focus map assigns exactly one focal voice per section.","Spectral check: in overlapping sections the two lowest voices do not share the same dominant band (e.g. low-band energy owned by one voice at a time)."],
 ["arrangement","ambient","variations","focus-map","frequency-slots","pad","texture","melody","structure"])

# 4 ---------------------------------------------------------------- 16-bar block structure
S("JHiTUBVLmFI","6 key concepts for arranging techno",
 "dynamic-interest-16-bar-block-map-top-perc-gear-shifts-break-turnaround-squeeze-in-markers-reverses-plus-minus-layers-scoop-up-minimalism",
 "Dynamic-interest block map for loop-based tracks: 10 x 16-bar blocks drawn on paper, top-percussion gear shifts at every block, one committed break vs half-block turnarounds, 1-3 bar squeeze-ins, sparing markers/reverses, +/- density from call-and-response or polymeter layers, scoop-ups and minimalism moments",
 "Arrange one finished groove into a ~5-minute track that keeps dynamic interest by contrast rather than new ideas. Draw 10 blocks of 16 bars (each ~30 s at dance tempos) and plan: top-percussion gear shifts at every block boundary; one break block (kick and low percussion out, return = drop) versus turnarounds in the second half of a block; squeeze-ins of 1-3 extra contrast bars mid-track; markers (splash at a block start) and reverses (build of fullness into a block end) used sparingly; +/++/- density marks filled by call-and-response or polymeter layers; scoop-ups (lows out for 1-4 bars at a block start) and minimalism moments (strip to kick + lead for 2-4 bars). Delta vs arrangement-loop-to-song cluster: the drawn 16-bar block canvas and named contrast toolbox, with a prep list of what to design before laying out.",
 ["A finished 16-bar core groove (kick/low end + main synth)","A DAW timeline, or pen and paper for the block drawing","Reverb for printing reverse swells"],
 [("core_loop","project","16-bar groove that already works on its own",True),("length_blocks","number","Number of 16-bar blocks (10 ~ 5 min at dance tempos)",False)],
 [("block_map","notes","Drawn map: per block gear shift, break/turnaround/squeeze-in, markers/reverses, +/- marks, scoop-ups, minimalism moments"),("prep_kit","audio","Top-percussion options, contrast material, reverse swells, call/response and polymeter layers"),("arrangement","project","Laid-out track")],
 ["Design all ingredients on the loop first; do not just copy the loop 10 times and hope.",
  "Keep intro and outro blocks DJ-friendly (whole 16-bar blocks); put squeeze-ins only in the middle.",
  "Do not put a crash/marker at every block start; it gets overdone.",
  "For Matt's ambient/cinematic use: pick softer, longer top-percussion options (rides, open hats, soft noise hit) over busy fast hat rolls, which can become a rapid clicking texture he dislikes.",EARLY,ABL],
 [("Finish one core groove you are happy with and design all the extra ingredients on it before spreading anything on the timeline.","Loop design","Complete ingredient list"),
  ("Draw the canvas: blocks of 16 bars (~30 s each at most dance tempos); a ~5-minute track is 10 blocks. Literally draw 10 stripes on paper.","Pen and paper","10-block road map"),
  ("Tool 1, top-percussion gear shifts: prepare many top-percussion options (closed and open hats, rides, near and far, short and long, a backbeat clap, a short noise hit around 500 Hz, a rim). At every block boundary add and/or remove some so each block has its own high-end profile.","Percussion options","Energy contour moves forward at each block"),
  ("Tool 2, breaks and turnarounds: mark one whole block as the break (e.g. block 6), removing the kick and low percussion and returning them at the drop. Mark turnarounds in the second half of some blocks (e.g. blocks 4 and 8): scoop out lows and/or add contrasting top material without interrupting the block flow. Prepare a contrasting timbre (effects that reset the sound world) for them.","Block map","Break and turnaround positions marked"),
  ("Squeeze-ins: in the middle of the track only, add 1-3 bars of contrast between blocks (e.g. after the first and last turnarounds). Prep by appending ~3 bars of silence after the 16-bar loop and designing contrast material there.","Loop + 3 empty bars","Surprise moments that break predictability"),
  ("Tool 3, markers and reverses: place a marker (cymbal or noise splash) at the start of only a few blocks, and reverses (a build of fullness, e.g. a synth opening filter and envelopes, or printed huge-reverb swells reversed) into a few block ends, e.g. into the break and before the last block. Print several reverse layers ahead of time, many in the highs, some in the mids.","Reverb print + reverse","Sparse, intentional section accents"),
  ("Tool 4, density marks: prepare a couple of call-and-response phrases (call, gap, response, small turnaround) and polymeter layers. Mark blocks with + (one layer), ++ (several) or - (stripped, e.g. intro/outro) and add or remove the layers accordingly.","Extra layers","Core groove shifts gear without new big ideas"),
  ("Tool 5, scoop-up and minimalism moments: at a block start, remove the lows for 1-4 bars and bring them straight back (scoop-up); after a dense passage, strip to the essentials (e.g. kick + lead, no hats) for 2-4 bars before everything returns.","Mutes / clip gaps","Contrast via removal"),
  ("Tool 6 (optional): duplicate the main synth and perform cutoff and envelope lengths by hand across the arrangement, keeping the loop version as a fallback. Then lay out the track from the drawn plan and adjust where theory and practice disagree.","MIDI controller + duplicate track","Final arrangement following the block map")],
 ["Arrangement length is a whole number of 16-bar blocks plus any marked squeeze-in bars.","Top-percussion layer set differs between every pair of adjacent blocks.","Exactly one block is marked as the break; markers appear on fewer than half of the block starts."],
 ["arrangement","structure","sections","breakdown","drop","turnaround","transitions","dance","loop-to-song"])

# 5 ---------------------------------------------------------------- async band-pass texture
S("haZ6byjMpOk","The Joy of Recording Episode 1: Evolving Ambient Textures",
 "async-dual-bandpass-sweep-micro-loop-texture-parallel-delays-octave-down-lane-slow-autopan-bus",
 "Evolving ambient bed from a captured micro-loop: two parallel band-pass lanes swept by unsynchronised slow LFOs, delays on each lane, an octave-down lane, all summed into a bus with a very slow, also unsynchronised auto-pan",
 "Turn a short played loop (guitar or any instrument captured in a micro-looper) into an organic, never-repeating ambient backing pad. Two parallel (not series) narrow band-pass filters each let through a small wedge of the spectrum ('a flashlight on a painting') and sweep slowly up and down at different, unsynchronised rates. Each lane runs through a delay, one lane also gets an octave-down effect, and both feed a third bus with a very slow auto-pan that is unsynchronised with both filters, so three out-of-time movements create endless variation. Delta vs the generative/noodle-wind variants: an audio-loop source (not noise) with two async band-pass lanes, an octave-down lane and an async auto-pan bus.",
 ["A looper or sampler to capture a short loop","Two band-pass filters with free-running (Hz, not synced) LFOs","Delay, octave-down pitch effect, auto-pan, reverb"],
 [("micro_loop","audio","Short loop of played material (guitar, keys, etc.)",True)],
 [("texture_bed","audio","Evolving ambient bed usable as a piece or as a backing pad to write over")],
 ["The transcript gives no numeric rates; choose slow, mutually unrelated free-running rates for the two filter LFOs and the auto-pan (avoid simple ratios so they never line up).",
  "Movement is filter, delay and pan only; the octave-down lane is a fixed -12 st, so pitch stays steady (Matt's preference).",
  "Keep delay feedback moderate so repeats smear rather than tick (no rapid clicking texture).",EARLY,ABL],
 [("Capture a small loop of played material in a micro-looper or sampler as the source nugget.","Looper","Short seamless loop"),
  ("Send it to two parallel lanes (side by side, not one into the other), each with a narrow band-pass filter that passes only a small wedge of the spectrum.","2 parallel band-pass lanes","Two thin, different slices of the loop"),
  ("Sweep each band-pass slowly up and down the spectrum with its own free-running LFO at a different, unsynchronised rate.","LFO -> band-pass frequency","Slices reveal different frequencies at different times"),
  ("Put a delay on each lane; on one lane also add an octave-down effect to extend the range downward.","Delay; octave-down","Wider 'sonic orchestra' with a lower register"),
  ("Route both lanes into a third bus with a very slow auto-pan whose rate is unrelated to both filter LFOs; add reverb.","Bus: auto-pan + reverb","Three out-of-time movements; the texture never repeats exactly"),
  ("Render a long take and use it as an ambient piece or as a bed to play grooves and melodies over.","Render","Evolving backing texture")],
 ["Spectral centroid of the rendered bed varies slowly over time with no exact repeat at the source loop length.","Pan position cycles slower than either filter sweep.","No pitch movement except the fixed octave-down layer."],
 ["texture","band-pass","asynchronous-modulation","micro-loop","auto-pan","octave-down","ambient","evolving"])
